#
# The chains
#

pmc:CastanedaToltecSitioPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Finding one's spot before learning about Mescalito"@en, "Den eigenen Platz finden, bevor man über Mescalito lernt"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:CastanedaToltecPeyoteIngestion ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:CastanedaToltecFindingTheSitio ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Unless the riddle of the spot is solved, the teacher will have nothing to say and the learner may as well leave; to quit is never to learn." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 15-17, entry of 25 June 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecIntentPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Intent beyond reproach before the smoke"@en, "Tadellose Absicht vor dem Rauch"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokingTheMixture ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:CastanedaToltecUnbendingIntent ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without intending and willing his return the smoker is not let back by the smoke, and without willing to remember what he saw it is only fog in his mind." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 56, entry of 27 January 1962; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecStrongLifePresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A strong life before and after the smoke"@en, "Ein starkes Leben vor und nach dem Rauch"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokingTheMixture ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:CastanedaToltecStrongQuietLife ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The smoke can be matched only with strength; without it a man's life is shattered to bits." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 46 and 103, entries of 23 November 1961 and 28 January 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecStayingOnSpotPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Staying on the spot to win the fight for the soul"@en, "Auf dem Platz bleiben, um den Kampf um die Seele zu gewinnen"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:CastanedaToltecFightingForm ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:CastanedaToltecFindingTheSitio ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Had he moved one inch from it he would have been struck down like a thunderbolt, his soul kept, and he would have wasted away." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 133 and 139, entry of 29 October 1965; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecRequisitesPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The four requisites before going to knowledge"@en, "Die vier Erfordernisse, bevor man zum Wissen geht"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:CastanedaToltecManOfKnowledge ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:CastanedaToltecFourRequisites ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Any other way of going to knowledge or to war is an error, and the one who commits it will come to regret his steps." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 32, entry of 20 August 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecShootSeedsPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The shoot must seed before the taming"@en, "Der Spross muss Samen tragen vor der Zähmung"@de ;
    skos:note "Don Juan says the learner may count himself ready only when the plant has seeded; the second portion is taught once the bush has seeded a year later. No consequence of skipping is named."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:CastanedaToltecLizardSorcery ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:CastanedaToltecReplantingTheShoot ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 42 and 53, entries of 10 September and 26 December 1961; Part One, p. 75, entry of 27 January 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecSecondPortionPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The second portion twenty times before the third"@en, "Der zweite Teil zwanzigmal vor dem dritten"@de ;
    skos:note "The second portion must be mastered and taken at least twenty times before the third step; the work does not name the third step, and the flight it calls a use of the second portion is entered here as the further step the text reaches."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:CastanedaToltecBodilyFlight ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:CastanedaToltecLizardSorcery ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 75, entry of 27 January 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecPipeBondPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The bond with the pipe before smoking"@en, "Die Verbindung mit der Pfeife vor dem Rauchen"@de ;
    skos:note "When the friendship with the pipe is made one will smoke from it; liking the pipe is said to help one be unafraid. No consequence of skipping is named, though the writer says he was made to smoke before he felt ready (p. 96)."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokingTheMixture ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:CastanedaToltecBefriendingThePipe ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 47, entry of 23 November 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecEscogidoPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Being chosen before the secrets"@en, "Auserwählt sein vor den Geheimnissen"@de ;
    skos:note "Don Juan says he could reveal his secrets to nobody until he found his chosen man."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:CastanedaToltecManOfKnowledge ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:CastanedaToltecEscogido ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 30-31, entry of 17 August 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

#
# The series
#

pmc:CastanedaToltecFourEnemies a pm:Sequencing ;
    rdfs:label "The four natural enemies of the man of knowledge"@en, "Die vier natürlichen Feinde eines Menschen des Wissens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four enemies, fear first, then clarity, power and finally old age, the enemies a learner meets in that order on the way to becoming a man of knowledge."@en ;
    skos:note "Ordered by don Juan himself; the analysis of Part Two repeats the order as the renewal of the quest (p. 150). No consequence of skipping an enemy is named, because none can be skipped, so no pm:Presupposing pairs are entered."@en ;
    rdf:_1 pmc:CastanedaToltecFear ;
    rdf:_2 pmc:CastanedaToltecClarity ;
    rdf:_3 pmc:CastanedaToltecPower ;
    rdf:_4 pmc:CastanedaToltecOldAge ;
    pm:seriesStatement "Each enemy is met only through victory over the one before it: clarity is what replaces vanquished fear, power is what clarity becomes when defied, and old age comes at the end of learning when power is held in check; the man of knowledge exists only in the moment the last is fought off." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 57-60, entry of 15 April 1962; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecFourHeads a pm:Sequencing ;
    rdfs:label "The four heads of the devil's weed"@en, "Die vier Köpfe des Teufelskrauts"@de ;
    skos:definition "Root, stem and leaves, flowers, seeds: the four parts of the Datura plant as don Juan orders their learning."@en ;
    skos:note "No consequence of skipping a head is named. The weed's own man takes only root and seeds, stem and leaves only in his own illness, flowers never."@en ;
    rdf:_1 pmc:CastanedaToltecRootHead ;
    rdf:_2 pmc:CastanedaToltecDaturaHealing ;
    rdf:_3 pmc:CastanedaToltecDaturaFlowers ;
    rdf:_4 pmc:CastanedaToltecSoberHead ;
    pm:seriesStatement "Whoever makes the weed his ally must learn the heads in this order, from the root through which her power is conquered to the seeds, the strongest and the only one that strengthens the heart, which the weed seeks to keep him from by killing him first." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 35-36, entry of 23 August 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecCrowStages a pm:Sequencing ;
    rdfs:label "The stages of becoming a crow"@en, "Die Stufen der Verwandlung in eine Krähe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Legs, then tail and wings, then the shrinking of the head, then the crow's eyes, before the flight."@en ;
    skos:note "Ordered by don Juan's commands under the smoke as the writer recalls them. The analysis takes the whole as hypnotic suggestion (p. 162). No consequence of skipping is named except that short wings will not fly."@en ;
    rdf:_1 pmp:CastanedaToltecCrowLegs ;
    rdf:_2 pmp:CastanedaToltecCrowTailAndWings ;
    rdf:_3 pmp:CastanedaToltecCrowHead ;
    rdf:_4 pmp:CastanedaToltecCrowEyes ;
    pm:seriesStatement "Each change is commanded only after the one before: the learner is not yet solid until the tail grows, cannot fly while the head is large, and the sight of the crow is the last change before he can be tossed into the air." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 121-122, entry of 7 February 1965; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

#
# The cautions
#

pmc:CastanedaToltecKernelCureCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The kernel kills the curer who cannot repel it"@en, "Das Korn tötet den Heiler, der es nicht abwehren kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "Few brujos dare to suck out a kernel, because one not strong enough to repel it takes it into himself and dies instead."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecSuckingTheKernel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Introduction, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecPowerObjectsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Power objects are toys beside an ally"@en, "Machtgegenstände sind Spielzeug neben einem Verbündeten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whoever takes such tools for supreme is a fool; next to an ally they are children's toys and pursuing them wastes time."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecPowerObjects ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Introduction, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecEnemySpotCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The enemy spot weakens and can kill"@en, "Der feindliche Platz schwächt und kann töten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beside the good spot lies its opposite, called the enemy, which weakens a man and may even cause his death; the writer felt terror there."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecFindingTheSitio ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 17-18, entry of 25 June 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecWeedDistortsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The devil's weed distorts men"@en, "Das Teufelskraut verzerrt die Menschen"@de ;
    skos:definition "She gives a taste of power before the heart is strong, makes men domineering, unpredictable and weak in their strength, keeps those who want power and rids herself of those who cannot handle it, and makes them her slaves; the price can be overcome but not avoided."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the teacher against the ally he teaches. The analysis adds that ritual is the only protection against her unpredictability, never enough, and that her user must wait for favourable omens (p. 155)."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecYerbaDelDiabloNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 35, 41 and 43, entries of 23 August and 10 September 1961; Part Two, pp. 152-153; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecWeedKillsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The weed kills her protégés early"@en, "Das Kraut tötet seine Schützlinge früh"@de ;
    skos:definition "She is hard with those she favours and seeks to kill them quickly, usually before they reach the secrets of the seeds; she nearly killed don Juan each time he used her."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecYerbaDelDiabloNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 36 and 75, entries of 23 August 1961 and 28 January 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecNameCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Names are for calling in need"@en, "Namen sind zum Rufen in der Not"@de ;
    skos:definition "The other names of the weed, and Mescalito's name, are not to be spoken in talk; a name is kept for calling the power in moments of great need, which come sooner or later."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecYerbaDelDiabloNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 36 and 73, entries of 23 August 1961 and 3 July 1962; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecShovelCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A shovel spoils the root"@en, "Eine Schaufel verdirbt die Wurzel"@de ;
    skos:definition "A root cut with a shovel or other foreign tool will not grow for its owner when replanted."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecDaturaDigging ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 37-38, entry of 3 September 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecPipeCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A mishandled pipe destroys its keepers"@en, "Eine falsch gehandhabte Pfeife vernichtet ihre Hüter"@de ;
    skos:definition "A mistake in passing the pipe makes it burst or shatter and would be the end of teacher and learner; it must never be knocked, never held with sweaty hands, used only alone and seen by nobody, and its keeper dies if it is lost or broken."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecBefriendingThePipe ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 46, 48 and 95, entries of 23 November 1961 and 23 December 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecMixtureCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The wrong mushroom kills"@en, "Der falsche Pilz tötet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mixture is deadly poisonous to all but the smoke's own protégé and cannot be made without a teacher; mushrooms of the wrong kind harm man and pipe, and some men have died of the foul smoke."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokeMixturePreparation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 46 and 55, entries of 23 November 1961 and 27 January 1962; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecImageSeenCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The image must not be seen"@en, "Das Bild darf nicht gesehen werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The protection is weakened for as long as anyone who has seen it remembers it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecProtectionImage ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 49, entry of 26 December 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecShootSecretCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nobody may know where the shoot grows"@en, "Niemand darf wissen, wo der Spross wächst"@de ;
    skos:definition "A stranger who sees the place could cripple or kill the owner through the shoot, so he must take it and flee if followed; not even the teacher may know."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecReplantingTheShoot ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 53, entry of 26 December 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecMescalitoHorrorCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Mescalito is horror to those he dislikes"@en, "Mescalito ist Grauen für die, die er nicht mag"@de ;
    skos:definition "The kind protector cannot be mocked; toward those he dislikes he can be sheer horror."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecMescalitoNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 62, entry of 30 January 1962; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecBagCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The bag of Mescalito must not touch the ground"@en, "Der Beutel mit Mescalito darf den Boden nicht berühren"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the bags of gathered buttons touch the ground or a bush, Mescalito will never again let himself be taken by that man; nor may one point at a plant."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecPeyoteGathering ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 64-65 and 71-72, entry of 6 July 1962; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecSongsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never another man's songs"@en, "Niemals die Lieder eines anderen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One must never imitate another man's songs or ask after them; whoever sings someone else's songs without knowing their sense is a phony."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecCallingMescalito ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 73 and 110, entries of 3 July 1962 and 10 September 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecPersonalQuestionCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "No personal questions to the lizards at first"@en, "Anfangs keine persönlichen Fragen an die Eidechsen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first questions must not concern oneself or people one knows; the longing to touch or speak to them stops the lizard and breaks the sorcery, and one must know much more before seeing things of one's own."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecLizardSorcery ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 80 and 85, entries of 21 and 18 April 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecHasteCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Haste in the lizard sorcery hurts"@en, "Eile bei der Eidechsenzauberei schadet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Acting too fast and without deliberation in the dance and the steps brings harm; the paste is a power of unknown kind and must be handled with respect."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecLizardSorcery ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 81, entry of 21 April 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecForeheadCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never the paste on the forehead"@en, "Niemals die Paste auf die Stirn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The paste must be kept off the middle of the forehead, which belongs to the great brujos alone."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecLizardSorcery ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 79-80 and 114-116, entries of 21 April 1963 and 24 December 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecLizardsInsideCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Lizards inside bind one to the weed"@en, "Eidechsen im Inneren binden an das Kraut"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the lizards come to live inside, the weed never lets one go; one spends one's life grooming her and is cut off from all else, so not finding them is better and leaves one free."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecEatingTheLizards ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 85-86, entry of 19 April 1963; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecWeedTrapCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The weed's trap is to seem the only path"@en, "Die Falle des Krauts ist, als einziger Weg zu erscheinen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Like a flattering woman she sets traps, gives a feeling of power and makes one believe hers is the only way; one must not take her with passion, and must choose free of fear and ambition, for the smoke blinds with fear and the weed with ambition."@en ;
    skos:note "Spoken in the first person as a warning (I warn you), twice."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecAskingThePathQuestion ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 76 and 117-118, entries of 28 January 1963 and 28 December 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecGuideCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The smoke without a guide kills"@en, "Der Rauch ohne Führer tötet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Handing the smoke to just anyone, with no guide, would be to kill him, because nobody would bring him back."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokingTheMixture ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 102, entry of 28 January 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecMirrorCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "No mirror or camera under the smoke"@en, "Kein Spiegel und keine Kamera unter dem Rauch"@de ;
    skos:definition "Looking at oneself in a mirror while under the smoke would probably kill one with fright, and a camera might turn the smoke against one; one learns how a man looks only when giving the smoke to another."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokingTheMixture ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 104, entry of 28 January 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecSecretsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Three secrets of the smoke are never told"@en, "Drei Geheimnisse des Rauchs werden nie verraten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The making of the mixture, the way of moving about and the way back are never to be revealed; other matters of the smoke may be told."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecSmokingTheMixture ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 104, entry of 28 January 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecFormsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Large and small forms are dangerous"@en, "Große und kleine Gestalten sind gefährlich"@de ;
    skos:definition "A lion or bear costs too much energy and draws notice; a cricket, lizard or ant is preyed upon; the crow is safe because nobody minds it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecBecomingACrow ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 129, entry of 10 April 1965; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecRiverCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Running water can carry off the soul-lost"@en, "Fließendes Wasser kann den Seelenlosen fortreißen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The noise of a stream or a river can catch one bewitched and robbed of his soul and bear him off to die."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecSoulLoss ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 132, entry of 29 October 1965; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecHurlCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hurl only when life is at stake"@en, "Nur werfen, wenn das Leben auf dem Spiel steht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The form is for extreme crisis only and the throw only in plain danger of death; the war cry stays for life and must be right from the first, so it is not used at random."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecHurlingWithWarCry ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 132, entry of 29 October 1965; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecHelperCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Beware a female helper and going too far"@en, "Hüte dich vor einer weiblichen Helferin und davor, zu weit zu gehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One should seek a helper who is male, so as not to fall prey to a diablera who makes one suffer; and one who returns is bound to go back ever farther from the entrance until one day he cannot return."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:CastanedaToltecCrossingTheCrack ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 138, entry of 29 October 1965; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecKnowledgeHarmCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Some knowledge harms the unready"@en, "Manches Wissen schadet dem Unbereiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Certain knowledge is too strong for the learner's strength and talk of it would only harm him; learning by conversation is waste."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecManOfKnowledge ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, p. 34, entry of 20 August 1961; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecRuleCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Breaking the rule is usually fatal"@en, "Die Regel zu brechen ist meist tödlich"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rule of an ally is fixed and cannot be added to; departing from it usually kills, and any attempt to change it is deadly; only the ally may, from affinity, hold back the effect."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated in the analysis as don Juan's inference from the writer's error with the forehead."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:CastanedaToltecAllyBeyondBoundaries ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part Two, pp. 156-157; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

#
# The author's own orderings
#

pmc:CastanedaToltecStructuralAnalysis a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The structural analysis: four units and a conceptual order"@en, "Die Strukturanalyse: vier Einheiten und eine begriffliche Ordnung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The writer orders the teachings as an operative order of four units, each modifying the one before: the man of knowledge; his ally; the ally's rule; and the rule's corroboration by special consensus given by the teacher; beneath it a conceptual order which the apprentice either adopts in good faith or only fallaciously, failure being abandonment under any of the four enemies."@en ;
    skos:note "The writer says the order of the four units is free of his own classifying devices but that the components of each are not, and that the meanings are rendered as he understood them (p. 143). The teacher never stated this order; it is the writer's."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Carlos Castaneda, in Part Two and the appendixes of the work" ;
    dcterms:date "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part Two, pp. 143-144 and 173-179; Appendix B, pp. 193-196; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecSevenThemes a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The seven themes of the man of knowledge"@en, "Die sieben Themen des Menschen des Wissens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Learning, unbending intent (frugality, judgment, no freedom to innovate), clarity of mind (freedom to choose a path, knowing the purpose, being fluid), strenuous labor (the drama of exertion, efficacy, meeting challenge), the warrior (respect, fear, wakefulness, self-confidence), the unceasing process (renewal, impermanence, the path with heart), and having an ally."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Carlos Castaneda, in Part Two and the appendixes of the work" ;
    dcterms:date "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part Two, pp. 144-150; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecSixPurposes a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The six specific purposes of the allies' rules"@en, "Die sechs besonderen Zwecke der Regeln der Verbündeten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Testing, divination and bodily flight with the Datura ally, by ingestion and by ingestion with absorption through the skin; testing, movement through objects and beings, and movement in an alternate form with the mushroom ally, by ingestion and inhalation; set in the order the teacher presented them."@en ;
    skos:note "The writer says flight was not corroborated in depth and movement through things only discussed; he holds that the crow technique included hypnotic suggestion besides the smoke (p. 162)."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Carlos Castaneda, in Part Two and the appendixes of the work" ;
    dcterms:date "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part Two, pp. 160-163; Appendix B, p. 195; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

pmc:CastanedaToltecManipulationOfCues a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The teacher's direction of the states by cues and emphasis"@en, "Die Lenkung der Zustände durch Hinweise und Betonung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The writer explains the coloured spots of the first night and the impersonation of the last as special states of ordinary reality produced by don Juan through cues about the surroundings and about his own behaviour, and the course of all states as directed by the teacher's positive, negative and withheld emphasis in the retelling, so that the states progress toward the specific, toward a wider range and toward practical use."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the work's own naturalising reading of two events that Part One reports as the finding of a spot of power and the attack of a diablera; it stands beside them and is not reconciled with them by the writer."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Carlos Castaneda, in Part Two and the appendixes of the work" ;
    dcterms:date "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CastanedaToltec ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part Two, pp. 164-173; Appendix A, pp. 181-191; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

#
# The protocol
#

pmc:CastanedaToltecLizardDivinationTest a pm:Testing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CastanedaToltecTeachingsOfDonJuan ;
    rdfs:label "Divination by the lizards"@en, "Wahrsagen durch die Eidechsen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the sorcery of the lizards shows the answer to a question about an absent person, a lost object or an unseen place."@en ;
    skos:note "The work gives the case and not its check: asked who stole books from a reading room, the writer sees a young man deface books and pack them in crates, and in the second sorcery a young man with papers; neither answer is said to have been compared with what happened. It also gives the escapes in advance: a wandering thought gets an answer to an unknown question, and a personal question breaks off the sorcery. A protocol must therefore fix the question, its wording and the means of checking before the sorcery, and record every attempt."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:CastanedaToltecLizardYielding ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Answers to fixed questions about matters unknown to the diviner, recorded before the facts are established, match the established facts no more often than guessed answers to the same questions." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Carlos Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan: A Yaqui Way of Knowledge (copyright 1968 by the Regents of the University of California), Part One, pp. 79-85 and 114-116, entries of 18 to 21 April 1963 and of 24 and 28 December 1964; read in the full-text layer of the omnibus The Teachings of Don Juan, A Separate Reality, Tales of Power (New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1995), whose first part carries the work on pp. 1-196" .

